{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Before YHWH.</b> In Torat Kohanim: What does the verse come to teach? Rabbi Nehemiah said: Since we find regarding the bull of Yom Kippur that he stands inside [i.e., on the inner side] of the altar and sprinkles upon the curtain — could one say the same here? Scripture comes to teach: ״הַמִּזְבֵּחַ לִפְנֵי ה׳״ — ״the altar before YHWH״ — and not ״the priest before YHWH״; thus far. This implies that if we had not found [this detail] regarding the bull of Yom Kippur, there would have been no need to tell us ״לִפְנֵי ה׳.״ And it is difficult: from where do I know his standing place? And since I do not know from the verse that he has a known place, we would come to say: if he wishes — he may stand inside the altar; if he wishes — he may stand outside the altar? Scripture comes to teach ״לִפְנֵי ה׳,״ to say that he must stand outside the altar. And why was it necessary to say ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״? Further it is difficult: from where is there place to derive that he sprinkles upon the curtain while he is outside the altar? Perhaps it comes for its own subject — that he sprinkles upon the altar while he is outside the altar, in the manner Scripture says regarding the bull of Yom Kippur (Leviticus 16:18): ״וְיָצָא אֶל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ,״ and in any case the sprinkling upon the curtain would be while he is inside." "And if we had nothing but this difficulty, there would be room to resolve it. However, I saw further in parashat Aharei Mot (Leviticus 16:18): A tanna taught: ״וְיָצָא אֶל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ״ — what does this come to teach? Rabbi Nehemiah said: Since we find regarding the bull brought for the commandments [i.e., the bull of the anointed priest] that he stands outside the altar and sprinkles upon the curtain — could one say the same here? Scripture comes to teach ״וְיָצָא,״ and where was he? ״לִפְנֵי ה׳,״ — thus far. It is difficult: what is the force of the question ״what does this come to teach?״? But it was necessary to say that he goes out from his place and does not sprinkle upon the altar while he is inside. Further, his saying ״since it is stated״ implies specifically because it was stated, but absent its having been stated, the verse would not inform us of his standing place when he sprinkles upon the curtain. And further, according to his words, he should not have framed it as the negation of ״וְיָצָא״ — from which he is compelled to say ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״ regarding the bull of the commandments, just as he reasons in his words: ״What does לִפְנֵי ה׳ come to teach, since we find …״ etc. — and not the negation of ״לִפְנֵי ה׳,״ from which he is compelled to say ״וְיָצָא״ in parashat the bull of Yom Kippur, as he says: ״What does וְיָצָא come to teach, since we find …״ etc. Further it is difficult, his saying: ״since we find regarding the bull brought for the commandments that he stands outside the altar and sprinkles upon the curtain״ — where do we find the verse saying so? And if from his saying ״לִפְנֵי ה׳,״ perhaps it is speaking of the sprinkling upon the altar, according to the plain sense of the verse, and not of the sprinkling upon the curtain. And further, let us consider: if he had not said either ״וְיָצָא״ or ״לִפְנֵי ה׳,״ where would the place of his standing be? And if so, how could the tanna say ״what does this come to teach?״ regarding both of them?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the tanna's derivation follows this order: had the verse not said either ״וְיָצָא״ or the extra ״לִפְנֵי ה׳,״ it is simple that we would judge that he may sprinkle either upon the curtain or upon the altar in any manner — whether the altar is inside or he is inside. And after the verse came and mentioned, regarding the bull for unwitting failure to perform a commandment [of the anointed priest], the inner altar, and the plain sense of the verse concerns the sprinkling upon the altar; and mentioned regarding the bull of Yom Kippur also an inner altar, as stated: ״וְיָצָא אֶל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ״ — in this particular detail the bull of Yom Kippur was equated with the bull of the commandments. It also informed us regarding the bull of Yom Kippur that the sprinkling upon the curtain takes place while he is inside, for from his saying ״וְיָצָא״ it is implied that he had been inside. Now reason dictates that the same law should apply to the bull of the commandments: the sprinkling upon the curtain should be while he is inside the altar, and it [this bull's verse] would learn what is missing from the bull of Yom Kippur. Consequently, the bull of Yom Kippur and the bull of the commandments would be equal: he sprinkles upon the curtain while inside, and he goes out and sprinkles upon the altar. Therefore the tanna came and removed this notion from the listener's mind through the force of the hermeneutical derivation, and said: ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״ — what does it come to teach? Meaning, after the verse stated regarding the bull of Yom Kippur ״וְיָצָא,״ you have learned that the sprinkling upon the altar takes place with the altar inside — if so, why was it necessary to say here ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״? If it comes to teach that he should not sprinkle upon the altar while he is outside, it is not needed, and he would learn everything from the bull of Yom Kippur. And Rabbi Nehemiah resolved that it was necessary to say ״לִפְנֵי ה׳,״ meaning: from the fact that we see the verse did not wish to learn the bull of the commandments from the bull of Yom Kippur, this points with a finger that it is not analogous to it. And in what respect is it not analogous? Because we find in it one particular that was not mentioned regarding the bull of the commandments, namely the sprinkling upon the curtain while he is inside. And that is his saying: ״since we find regarding the bull of Yom Kippur that he stands inside and sprinkles upon the curtain,״ and if I were to derive the bull of the commandments from it, I would be able to say this as well — Scripture comes to teach ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״ etc. Henceforth his saying ״the altar before YHWH״ excludes ״the priest before YHWH״ in all the sprinklings, whether upon the altar or upon the curtain — for what basis does the verse rely upon to indicate where his standing place is during the sprinkling upon the curtain? If we learn from the bull of Yom Kippur, we would learn everything as well; rather, it certainly acquired its place [from] ״the altar before YHWH״ and not ״the priest before YHWH״ in all the sprinklings stated in this matter. And regarding the verse ״וְיָצָא״ stated regarding Yom Kippur, he derived along this path: what does it come to teach? Meaning, the implication of the plain sense of the verse is that he should not sprinkle upon the altar until he goes out, and it also informs us that previously, when he sprinkled upon the curtain, he was inside." "<b>Now behold,</b> in the first derivation [i.e., the baraita on ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״ in our verse] it is heard from the verse that one must go out to sprinkle upon the altar, but regarding the sprinkling upon the curtain, which requires standing inside, there is no necessity in the matter, and he may sprinkle whether he is inside or outside. And since he has permission to stand wherever he wishes to sprinkle upon the curtain, the verse warned that when he finishes inside [to sprinkle] upon the curtain, he should go out to sprinkle upon the altar. For this reason the tanna came and stated the derivation for whose sake he built the question, namely: ״since we find regarding the bull brought for the commandments that he stands״ etc. — meaning, even though it was not mentioned there except in connection with the sprinkling upon the altar, and we did not learn about the sprinkling upon the curtain except from the fact that it did not learn from the bull of Yom Kippur and it was necessary to say ״לִפְנֵי ה׳,״ nevertheless, according to what we are presently reasoning — namely, if it were not stated regarding the bull of Yom Kippur ״וְיָצָא״ but only ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״ which is in the bull for unwitting failure [of the anointed priest] — and given this, when I come to reason about his standing place during the sprinkling upon the curtain, I would say: if he wishes he stands inside, if he wishes he stands outside — for the verse was strict only about the sprinkling upon the altar, which requires that he stand outside, but during the sprinkling upon the curtain there is no impediment in the matter. You would then conclude that he may stand outside the altar and sprinkle upon the curtain. And that is his saying: ״since we find … that he stands outside the altar and sprinkles upon the curtain, could the same be so here as well? Scripture comes to teach וְיָצָא״ — meaning, as a requirement, that he must stand inside during the sprinkling upon the curtain; for if you were to say it is optional, he would not have needed to state it, and I would learn from the bull for unwitting failure. You have thereby learned that by force of his saying ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״ in the bull for unwitting failure, you are compelled to say that his saying ״וְיָצָא״ in the bull of Yom Kippur comes to teach that he must stand inside during the sprinkling upon the curtain — for if it were optional, he would not have needed to state it, and he would learn from the bull for unwitting failure two things: that he must stand outside when sprinkling upon the altar, and when sprinkling upon the curtain he may stand wherever he wishes. You have thereby learned from his saying ״וְיָצָא״ two things: that he must stand inside when he sprinkles upon the curtain, and he must stand outside when he sprinkles upon the altar. According to this, the difficulties that the Rav, Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi, left unresolved in parashat Aharei Mot (Leviticus 16:18) in his derivation of ״וְיָצָא״ are settled, and see his words there." "<b>However,</b> this requires investigation: according to this baraita, which established the requirement of standing inside during the sprinkling upon the curtain from the fact that it did not learn the bull of Yom Kippur from the bull for unwitting failure — if so, when we return to understand the baraita of ״לִפְנֵי ה׳ — why is it stated?״ etc., it is difficult — for surely ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״ is necessary to know, since without it I would not have known, for the intent of his saying ״וְיָצָא״ is to require standing inside during the sprinkling upon the curtain, as mentioned. Hence we return to what would have occurred to us to say: that even regarding the bull for unwitting failure, he would stand inside during the sprinkling upon the curtain, in the order stated regarding the bull of Yom Kippur. What will you say — why was it necessary to state it? It was necessary in order to reveal regarding the bull of Yom Kippur that it is an obligation to stand inside during the sprinkling upon the curtain, as stated, for absent this I would have said it is optional. And one may say that the tanna holds that ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״ does not come to make this derivation alone, namely to raise the question: he should learn from it regarding the bull of Yom Kippur, and from the fact that he did not learn etc., as mentioned — for this is not the way of the verse, since in any case it should be needed to teach something for its own sake; for if the intent of HaShem were only to reveal [something] about ״וְיָצָא,״ as mentioned, He should have explained the matter in its place regarding the bull of Yom Kippur." "<b>Further,</b> it appears to say: since this derashah is valid when we first ask why it says ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״ and derive it from ״וְיָצָא״ — why should this derashah be rejected in favor of the other? Therefore we learn both."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {2 "The Or HaChaim is analyzing the logic of the baraita in Torat Kohanim (Sifra) regarding the mutual derivations between the bull of the anointed priest (Lev 4) and the bull of Yom Kippur (Lev 16). I have translated לְפִי שֶׁמָּצִינוּ as 'since we find' throughout, preserving the tanna's technical hermeneutical formula.", 3 "The reference to 'the first derivation' (דְּרָשָׁה רִאשׁוֹנָה) is to the baraita on ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״ in comment [0] above, as distinct from the baraita on ״וְיָצָא״ in comment [1]."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:53:32.739079735Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}